  const first = rows[0], last = rows[rows.length - 1];
  return {
    slug: g.slug,
    title: `How Much RAM Does a ${g.name} Server Need? (By Player Count)`,
    excerpt: `${g.name} server RAM, CPU and disk for ${first.n} to ${last.n} players, from the same sizing model as our free calculator.`,
    coverEmoji: g.emoji,
    tags: [g.name.toLowerCase(), "ram", "server requirements", "sizing"],
    seoTitle: `How Much RAM Does a ${g.name} Server Need? (2026)`,
    seoDescription: `${g.name} dedicated server RAM by player count: ${formatRam(first.r.ramMB)} for ${first.n} players up to ${formatRam(last.r.ramMB)} for ${last.n}, plus CPU and disk.`,
    published: true,
    body: `**Short answer:** a ${g.name} server wants **${formatRam(first.r.ramMB)} of RAM for ${first.n} players** and **${formatRam(last.r.ramMB)} for ${last.n}**. ${w.hint}

## ${g.name} server requirements by player count

${table}

These figures come from the sizing model behind our [server size calculator](/tools/server-size-calculator?workload=${g.id}&players=${g.counts[1]}), so the table and the tool always agree. They are planning estimates for a typical server, not benchmarks: heavy mods, a large explored world or lots of automation can need more, and the calculator tells you when a requirement exceeds our largest plan.

## What changes the number

- **Players.** RAM grows with concurrent players; the table shows how much.
- **Mods and world size.** Both add to the base figure. Give a modded server a tier of headroom.
- **CPU.** Many game servers lean on single-thread speed, so a fast core matters as much as extra memory.

## Get the exact plan and price

Open the calculator with ${g.name} preselected: [${g.name} at ${g.counts[1]} players](/tools/server-size-calculator?workload=${g.id}&players=${g.counts[1]}). It maps the requirement to a real Nxeon plan with the live price. Or go straight to [${g.name} server hosting](${g.route}).`,
  };
}

